# === Basics ==================================================================
# Public URL of the app (locally http://localhost:3000, your domain in prod).
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Environment: development | staging | production (for labelling only).
# All environments use the same live products on Digistore24 (see
# docs/environments.md). Receive IPNs locally: node run.mjs ds24-tunnel (Cloudflare).
APP_ENV=development

# === Database (Postgres) =====================================================
# Locally via docker-compose (see docker-compose.yml). In prod: managed Postgres
# (Railway, Render, Fly.io and DigitalOcean all offer one next to the app —
# docs/DEPLOY.md, or ask Claude Code for the skill setup-hosting).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://app:app@localhost:15432/app
# How the LOCAL database is started. You do not have to set this: on the first
# start your machine is looked at, and the answer is written in here.
#   docker  Postgres 16 in a container (docker-compose.yml) — chosen whenever
#           there is a Docker that answers. It is the same Postgres your app
#           runs against on the server later.
#   local   Postgres 16 from an npm package, no Docker needed (about 60 MB, is
#           fetched on first use). Chosen on every machine without Docker.
# The second one is the second-best way, not a worse database: it is the real
# PostgreSQL 16, so migrations, scripts and DATABASE_URL are unaffected. What
# differs is that your local setup then deviates a little from production.
# Once the line is here it stays put, and that is deliberate — a Docker Desktop
# that did not start this morning looks exactly like a machine that never had
# one, and re-deciding would point your project at a second, empty database.
# Change it by hand only while the database holds nothing you want to keep.
# Anything other than these two values is refused (scripts/db/driver.mjs).
# DB_DRIVER=
# Port on which the local Docker Postgres is reachable. 15432 instead of the
# usual 5432, so that an already installed Postgres or another project is not
# in the way. If that one is taken as well, `node run.mjs start` finds the next free
# port itself and enters it here and in DATABASE_URL.
# DB_PORT=15432
# Size of the DB connection pool per process (default 10). On a single
# server 10–20 is fine; on serverless keep it low + use a connection pooler.
# DB_POOL_MAX=10
# The two accounts `node run.mjs db-seed` creates for development (defaults
# owner@example.com and customer@example.com). Your own addresses belong here,
# not in front of the command — `VAR=… node run.mjs db-seed` is shell syntax
# that PowerShell does not have. See docs/database.md → Seed data.
# SEED_OWNER_EMAIL=
# SEED_MEMBER_EMAIL=

# === Auth.js =================================================================
# Locally this is filled in by `node run.mjs start`. For STAGING/PROD generate
# one in the host's secret management:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
AUTH_SECRET=
# Needed behind a proxy/PaaS so that the dynamic host is accepted.
AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true

# === Cron ====================================================================
# Bearer token that guards POST /api/cron — the way IN to the scheduled jobs
# from outside the app (docs/cron.md). Without it that endpoint refuses to run
# at all, so it can never be left open as a "delete my data" URL.
#
# You do NOT need this for the jobs to run. The app schedules them itself while
# it is up; this is only for a host that would rather do the timing, and for
# `node run.mjs cron`, which generates one here on first use.
# Random secret:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# CRON_SECRET=

# Optional product name (appears in the subject/body of the sign-in mail).
# APP_NAME=My App

# Name of the app in the interface (sidebar, browser tab, home page).
# Without this value the default from lib/app.ts applies. NEXT_PUBLIC_ means:
# the value is in the browser too — so only the name here, never a secret.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=My App

# --- Development login (DEV only) ---
# As long as NO mail delivery is configured and APP_ENV=development applies (and
# APP_URL points at localhost), you can sign in without a magic link and
# without a password — so that you can try the app out right away.
# As soon as Postmark or SMTP is set up, it disappears automatically.
# In STAGING/PROD it never exists; there mail delivery is mandatory and the app
# does not even start without it. To set it up: node run.mjs mail-setup
# Hard off switch:
# DEV_LOGIN=off

# --- Sign-in (default): email token / magic link ---
# Configure exactly ONE mail transport — Postmark OR SMTP.
# Sender address (fallback if no transport-specific one is set below).
EMAIL_FROM=login@your-domain.de
#
# Option A: Postmark (server token + verified sender/sender signature)
# POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN=
# POSTMARK_SENDER=login@your-domain.de
# POSTMARK_MESSAGE_STREAM=outbound
#
# Option B: SMTP (any mail server / mailbox)
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.yourprovider.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false           # true = Port 465 (SSL), false = 587 (STARTTLS)
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM=login@your-domain.de

# --- Sign-in (optional): Google OAuth ---
# Setup + approval take time (Google reviews the app). Guide: docs/auth-setup.md
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# === Digistore24 =============================================================
# What is the SAME for every installation is not here but in
# lib/digistore/config.mjs: the API address, the developer key of the template,
# the permissions it asks for and the public redirect page. Only what belongs to
# YOUR installation lives here.
#
# The API key and IPN passphrase belong in here — the app reads them from the
# environment (lib/digistore/settings.ts). There is deliberately NO interface to
# enter them: the key belongs to the installation, not to a user.
#
# The connection wizard writes both values here itself:
#
#   node run.mjs ds24-connect
#
# It opens the browser, you grant access at Digistore24, and the script fetches
# the key. None of this has to be entered by hand:
# DIGISTORE_API_KEY=
#
# The IPN passphrase (SHA512 check of incoming events) and a stable domain_id
# for the IPN hookup are written here by `node run.mjs ds24-sync` itself —
# do not set them by hand:
# DIGISTORE_IPN_PASSPHRASE=
# DIGISTORE_IPN_DOMAIN_ID=
#
# Products are managed centrally in config/digistore-products.json; the
# productIds are written in there by scripts/ds24/sync-products.mjs (not here).
#
# For the product approval (go-live): reseller/marketplace where approval is
# requested. Optional — without a value, node run.mjs ds24-approval derives the reseller
# from the language (German → Germany/1, otherwise USA/2; overridable via
# --lang/--reseller/--siteowner). Set this only if you need a specific marketplace.
# APP_LANG=de
# DIGISTORE_SITEOWNER_ID=

# === AI providers ============================================================
# The five AI companies this app can talk to. You need AT MOST ONE of them, and
# which one is a decision you make per task rather than per app — see
# `lib/ai/providers/` and `config/ai-models.json`.
#
# UNCOMMENT ANY ONE OF THE FIVE — that is the whole setup. The app ships bound
# to "auto", which means "run on whichever key is here", so the one you pick is
# the one it uses, on that company's current default model. You do not have to
# choose a company up front, and you are not stuck with the one you pick.
#
# There is nothing else to switch on: a provider with a key is available, one
# without is not, and a task bound BY NAME to a provider with no key is refused
# by name rather than failing at a customer's first request. What runs where,
# and what one call costs: node run.mjs ai-check
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# GEMINI_API_KEY=...
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=...
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
#
# The in-app assistant runs on one of these keys too — on whichever provider her
# `chat` task resolves to (config/ai-models.json → tasks.chat, "auto" as
# shipped). WITHOUT any key the chat page stays reachable but shows a notice
# instead of a chat; it never half-works and never fails silently.
#
# Whether the app has an assistant AT ALL is not decided here: that is
# `config/ai-chat.json` ("enabled"), because it is a property of the product and
# not of the machine. Both have to hold — see lib/ai/chat-config.ts. She is
# billed per message; what one answer costs is in docs/ai-chat.md.

# Time zone used to RENDER dates and times to users. Pinned so a page is
# server-rendered and hydrated in the same zone — otherwise the first component
# showing a clock time mismatches on hydration. Data is stored in UTC either
# way; this only affects display.
APP_TIME_ZONE=Europe/Berlin

# Once a day the session greeting asks the public template repo on GitHub
# (digistore-io/ds24-appkit) whether its GUIDANCE (CLAUDE.md, docs/,
# .claude/skills/) has been improved since this app was copied — one GET of one
# public file, no query string, no body, nothing about you or your app, and no
# server of ours involved. `off` switches it off entirely;
# `node run.mjs update` keeps working by hand. Details: docs/updates.md
# TEMPLATE_UPDATE_CHECK=off
